---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'FAQ | agediscriminationcalc.com';
const description = 'Frequently asked questions about ADEA age discrimination claims, filing deadlines, willfulness, and damages.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
  <h2>Who is protected by the ADEA?</h2>
  <p>Workers age 40 and older employed by companies with 20 or more employees. Federal government employees, state and local government employees, and union members are also covered under related statutes.</p>
  <h2>What is the EEOC filing deadline?</h2>
  <p>180 days from the discriminatory act (300 days in states with a fair employment agency). Missing this deadline bars your federal claim. After receiving a right-to-sue letter, you have 90 days to file in court.</p>
  <h2>What is the difference between back pay and liquidated damages?</h2>
  <p>Back pay compensates for actual lost wages. Liquidated damages double the back pay automatically when the violation is willful &mdash; no need to prove additional harm. Liquidated damages cannot exceed the back pay amount.</p>
  <h2>Does the ADEA cover independent contractors?</h2>
  <p>Generally no &mdash; the ADEA covers &ldquo;employees,&rdquo; not independent contractors. However, courts apply an economic reality test, and misclassified workers may still qualify.</p>
  <h2>Can I sue my employer directly without going to the EEOC first?</h2>
  <p>You must file an EEOC charge and receive a right-to-sue letter (or wait 60 days after filing) before suing under the ADEA. This is a mandatory step for private-sector claims.</p>
  <h2>Is age discrimination hard to prove?</h2>
  <p>ADEA claims require proving that age was the &ldquo;but-for&rdquo; cause of the adverse action (<em>Gross v. FBL Financial Services</em>, 557 U.S. 167 (2009)) &mdash; a higher standard than Title VII&rsquo;s mixed-motive framework. However, circumstantial evidence (pattern of replacing older workers, age-related comments, statistical disparities) can satisfy this standard.</p>
  <h2>What if I signed a severance agreement waiving my ADEA rights?</h2>
  <p>ADEA waivers require: 21 days to consider (45 days in group terminations), 7-day revocation period after signing, specific acknowledgment language, and written advice to consult an attorney. Waivers failing these requirements are not enforceable.</p>
</BaseLayout>
